{
  "result": "At dawn, city windows brighten one by one, as if the buildings are waking from a shared dream. A delivery truck rattles over a pothole, and somewhere above the street, a kettle begins to whistle.\n\nI like this hour because it belongs to nobody completely. The hurried commuters have not yet claimed the sidewalks, shops are still arranging their displays, and even the pigeons seem briefly thoughtful. A quiet morning makes ordinary things look ceremonial: a red scarf on a chair, a coin shining near the curb, a rectangle of sunlight resting on a wall.\n\nPerhaps attention is a kind of gratitude. When we notice small details, we give them a temporary home in our minds. We do not need mountains or miracles to feel astonished; sometimes it is enough to watch steam disappear above a cup of tea.\n\nBy midmorning, the city will become louder and more demanding. Phones will ring, deadlines will multiply, and people will hurry toward destinations they may barely remember choosing. Still, the early light will remain somewhere, stored like a bookmark between the pages of the day. If we pause later, even for a breath, we may find it again. Perhaps that is why mornings feel hopeful: they offer no promises, only possibilities, and ask nothing except that we arrive awake.\n\nThe world does not become gentler because we observe it, but observation can make us gentler within it. That seems a modest beginning, and beginnings are often enough.",
